<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Alba Longa</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.) · capital Alba Longa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Legendary mother city of the Latins in the Alban Hills, remembered by the Romans as the home of Romulus and Remus and the seat of the Latin festival.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Roman tradition held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascanius">Ascanius</a>, son of the Trojan hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, founded Alba Longa about thirty years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a>, and placed its foundation in the 12th century BCE. Archaeology shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> villages and cemeteries around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albano">Alban Lake</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cavo">Monte Cavo</a> from about 1000 BCE, but no single city has been firmly identified.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> described a line of Alban kings, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silvius">Silvius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a> and his usurping brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amulius">Amulius</a>, but these are legendary. What is more secure is the role of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> as a religious centre for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> peoples: the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cavo">Monte Cavo</a> hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feriae-latinae">Feriae Latinae</a>, a shared festival that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> kept celebrating long after Alba disappeared.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a> defeated Alba after the duel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatii-and-curiatii">Horatii and Curiatii</a> in the mid-7th century BCE, razed the city and moved its people to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelian-hill">Caelian Hill</a>. Leading Roman families such as the Julii claimed Alban descent, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-league">Latin League</a> inherited its religious role.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1150 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:36.82%;width:9.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alba Longa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-900&amp;focus=alba-longa" aria-label="Alba Longa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alba Longa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5308 947L5304 942M5308 947L5309 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5309 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-900&amp;focus=alba-longa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
